.TH BCC09-CC1 1 2026-11-12 "bcc09 0.16.2" "bcc09 Manual"
.SH NAME
bcc09\-cc1 \- the compiler proper of bcc09 (cc1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I prefix\c
.B /libexec/bcc09/cc1
.RB [ \-ptuwdOP [ \- ]]
.RB [ \-E
.RB [ \-D\c
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-U\c
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-I\c
.IR dir ]]
.RB [ \-o
.IR output ]
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cc1
compiles preprocessed C (as
.BR bcc09\-cpp (1)
and
.BR bcc09\-unproto (1)
leave it) to 6809 assembly for
.BR as09 (1),
from
.I file
(or standard input) to
.I output
(or standard output).
It is dev86's bcc compiler, for the 6809;
.BR bcc09 (1)
runs it, and its
.B \-C
option passes options to it.
.PP
A function's first argument is passed in X (a long's in D and X), and
the others on the stack, above the return address, which the caller
removes; a float is passed as a double, and a double always on the
stack.
An int or pointer result is in X, a long's or a float's in D and X (the
high word in D), and a double's in D, X, Y and U.
The caller saves the registers it needs: a function may change them
all (but U under
.BR \-u ,
the data's).
The C library's README describes the conventions in full.
.PP
A global or static variable declared
.B __direct
goes in segment 2, which
.BR ld09 (1)
keeps in one page, and is reached as
.BI < name
(with
.BR \-u ,
too; its address is still
.BI # name
or
.IB name ,U\c
); the start-up code sets DP to that page.
It can't be a function, typedef, parameter, or automatic or register
variable, and must be
.B __direct
in all of a variable's declarations or none.
The
.B extern __direct
variables the code refers to are imported in segment 2, so that
.BR ld09 (1)
refuses to link a module that defines one elsewhere.
.PP
Its own preprocessor
.RB ( \-E )
is what
.B "bcc09 \-e"
uses in place of
.BR bcc09\-cpp (1).
.SH OPTIONS
A flag option is turned off by following it with
.BR \- .
.TP
.B \-p
Position-independent code: functions and string constants reached
relative to the program counter, calls with
.BR LBSR .
.TP
.B \-u
That, and the data reached through U, linked at 0 (OS-9's way;
.B "bcc09 \-Mo"
gives it): U is then not used for values, and a static variable that
holds an address is set by code that runs before main.
.TP
.B \-t
Put each line of the C source in the assembly, as a comment.
.TP
.B \-d
Put the compiler's expression trees in the assembly, as comments.
.TP
.B \-w
Print the location counter after each line (and don't make branches
short).
.TP
.B \-O
Define
.BR __OPTIMISED__ ;
it doesn't change the code for the 6809 (the driver's
.B \-O
runs
.BR bcc09\-copt (1)).
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-f
Callers save registers, and the first argument goes in a register:
always so for the 6809.
.B \-c\-
and
.B \-f\-
(callee saves, the first argument on the stack) are errors: the C
library and its helpers are built for these conventions.
.TP
.B \-E
Preprocess only.
.TP
.BI \-D name
.TQ
.BI \-U name
.TQ
.BI \-I dir
With
.BR \-E ,
as
.BR bcc09\-cpp (1)
takes them.
.TP
.B \-P
With
.BR \-E ,
no line numbers.
.TP
.BI \-o " output"
The output file.
.SH MACROS
With
.BR \-E ,
cc1 defines
.B __BCC__
and
.BR __AS09__ ,
and
.B __CALLER_SAVES__
with
.BR \-c ,
.B __FIRST_ARG_IN_X__
with
.B \-f
and
.B __POS_INDEPENDENT__
with
.BR \-p ;
.B __FILE__
and
.B __LINE__
are built in.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0, or 1 if there were errors (given in the output, as a
.B fail
line and a comment, and on standard error).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bcc09 (1),
.BR bcc09\-cpp (1),
.BR bcc09\-unproto (1),
.BR bcc09\-copt (1),
.BR as09 (1)
